.TH FMT_STRM_INTERNAL 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
fmt_strm_internal \- Internal function for formatting a string stream

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/fmt.h>

.IB size_t
.IB fmt_strm_internal(char* dest, ...);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBfmt_strm_internal\fR function is an internal function used for formatting a string stream. It takes a destination buffer and a variable number of arguments, each of which is a string to be appended to the buffer.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB char*
A pointer to the destination buffer.
.IB ...
A variable number of arguments, each a pointer to a string to be appended.

The function returns the total number of bytes written to the destination buffer, or \fB(size_t)-1\fR if an overflow occurs.

The \fBfmt_strm_internal\fR function is intended to be used internally by the library to format strings efficiently.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBfmt_strm_internal\fR function to format a string stream:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/fmt.h>

int main() {
    char dest[100];
    const char* s1 = "Hello";
    const char* s2 = "World";

    size_t written = fmt_strm_internal(dest, s1, s2, NULL);

    if (written != (size_t)-1) {
        printf("Written %d bytes: %s\n", written, dest);
    } else {
        printf("Error: Buffer overflow\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
.nef